module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitter_element_container_lowering_is_keyed_by_leaf_spelling

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { AtCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { StructurallyGuaranteed }

data emitter_element_container_lowering_is_keyed_by_leaf_spelling: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitter_element_container_lowering_is_keyed_by_leaf_spelling" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder, standing: AtCeiling { rung: StructurallyGuaranteed, ceiling_reason: "structurally guaranteed at the type position; the residual is the leaf-spelling key at the callable-field tier, recorded below." as NonEmptyStr } },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a declared carrier alias and the canonical spelling of the carrier it names are ONE carrier, so they lower to ONE Rust type (DESIGN section 3: the declared shape and the realized shape are the same carrier, not two, and the emitter never asks which to use). The renderer's element-container decision read the host container off the ALIAS roster -- node_is_collection over is_declared_container_alias_spelling -- which answers for `Set` and misses the canonical name, because std.algebra deliberately registers `FinitePowerSet` with container_roster_name and no container_alias_row (canonical_spelling_sorts_ahead_of_alias_reason: the canonical name sorts ahead of the alias, so giving it an alias row would silently make it the emitted canonical spelling). One carrier was therefore emitted twice: the target OrdSet/BTreeSet realization wherever a position was spelled `Set`, and the std.algebra profile RECORD wherever the same position was spelled `FinitePowerSet`.",
    "IT DIVIDED THE ALIAS FROM ITS OWN RIGHT-HAND SIDE. std.types declares `type Set<element> = FinitePowerSet<element>`, and the alias renderer (v1.compiler.emit_rust render_rust_decl_type_or_base, the same site #14880 closed for Map) asks the very predicate that missed the canonical name -- so std_types.rs carried `pub type Set<Element> = Rc<crate::std_algebra::FinitePowerSet<Element>>;` while every `Set` position lowered to `Rc<BTreeSet<Element>>`. The std.algebra profile record agreed with the alias: its fields read `union: Rc<dyn Fn(Rc<FinitePowerSet<T>>) -> Rc<FinitePowerSet<T>>>` where the keyed sibling, after #14880, reads `insert: Rc<dyn Fn(K, V) -> Rc<HashMap<K, V>>>`.",
    "THE BINDINGS THEN DIVERGED TOO, AND THE DIVERGENCE WAS PINNED RATHER THAN FIXED. v1_type_expr_is_finite_power_set_carrier -- the predicate the supplemental bound rows read (extdeps.languages.rust_derive_contracts rust_btree_set_supplemental_generic_bound_rows: the OrdSet element owes its generic Ord + Clone) -- carried `node_is_collection` as a conjunct, added in #15512 with the note that the canonical spelling 'renders the structural record and owes no Ord'. It was true then, and it made the pair self-sealing: dag/gunbc/instruments/native_emission_controls.dag carried `control_forward_power_over_opaque`, a fn over a canonical-spelled NON-Ord element, held green BECAUSE the lowering was missing. The free-monoid carrier's predicate never carried that conjunct, which is why a `FreeMonoid<K>` payload paid its Clone on arrival and the finite carrier did not.",
    "THE FIXTURE THAT WOULD HAVE ANSWERED IT WAS OUT OF THE PR THAT FOUND IT (gunbc#15512 handoff): a payload spelled `FinitePowerSet<Int>` handed to a formal spelled `Set<Int>` -- the pair the alias says are one type -- was E0308, 'expected FinitePowerSet<i64>, found BTreeSet<i64>'. Not a seed refusal: rustc refusing emitted Rust, which is the floor this project exists to close. Same class, same shape as emitter_keyed_container_lowering_is_keyed_by_leaf_spelling, one algebra over: there the keyed half paid 32 E0308 in the regenerated std_algebra.rs at #14880's landing.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor -- the same carrier at two type positions, and a lawful program (an alias-typed argument at its own carrier's formal) refused by the host compiler. CURRENT RUNG after this change: structurally guaranteed at the type position. v1.compiler.infer_types node_is_finite_power_set_algebra_spelling admits the canonical spelling into node_is_collection, reading which spellings name the algebra from std.algebra's roster exactly as node_is_keyed_algebra_spelling does for the keyed half, and the conjunct comes out of v1_type_expr_is_finite_power_set_carrier so the bound set is decided by the same roster read -- spelling and bound are one decision now, and control_forward_power_over_opaque is replaced by control_canonical_set_size, which holds only because the two spellings lower to one type. CEILING: structurally guaranteed at the type position; the residue below keeps it from being fully so.",
    "THE FORK HAD FIVE SITES, NOT ONE, AND EACH NAMED THE SPELLING IN ITS OWN WAY. Closing the renderer's predicate alone moved the field position and left four behind, each found by emitting the pair and diffing. (i) The declared-record arm of v1.compiler.emit_rust render_rust_type_without_applied_binding and the authored-base arm of render_rust_fn_sig_type both tested `is_container_type` -- the ARITY roster, which algebra.dag's `arity_row_is_alias_spellings_only_reason` keeps alias-only on purpose -- so a canonical-spelled application at a fn parameter, return, or nested argument position was rendered as the std.algebra record while the field arm a few lines away lowered the same carrier to `Rc<BTreeSet<K>>`; each arm now also asks the arm below's own predicate. (ii) v1.compiler.emit_rust rust_receiver_has_callable_method_field took the callable-field tier for a canonical-spelled receiver: the profile record declares count/contains/member as arrow members, so it emitted `(members.count)()` against a BTreeSet (E0609), the keyed tier's exact failure at #15037; that tier now asks the same target fact the type renderer branches on (keyed_container_has_target_inhabitant). (iii) The fn-field census in v1.compiler.infer_emit_info: `has_fn_fields` is a REALIZATION question -- a record whose field holds a function value cannot derive Debug/PartialEq, one whose fields are values can -- and it was read off the declared shape, so a canonical-spelled carrier field, resolving to the carrier's callable method surface, marked its holder as fn-carrying and cost it the derives and the container's hand-written impls: `==` over two canonical-spelled records was E0369 with the alias-spelled pair comparing green beside it. (iv) The same census REACHES through field_type_map, where the alias named no summary and stopped the walk while the canonical name named std.algebra's own record and propagated. Both now ask the roster (field_type_is_realized_container), so a carrier the target realizes is terminal and a carrier it does not -- `PartialFunction<K, V>`, `PointwisePower<T>` -- still propagates and stays on its record. (v) The Clone half of the supplemental bound rows read `is_container_type` again, in v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit v1_field_type_expr_needs_clone_bound_for_param_narrow, so after (i)-(iv) the canonical spelling paid its element's Ord and not its Clone: E0277 on the `Rc<BTreeSet<K>>` field with the alias-spelled struct emitting `<K: Clone>`.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the lowering still asks the LEAF SPELLING, not the declaration identity std.algebra.FinitePowerSet -- the emitter reaches only the authored spelling at these sites. The resolver records a DeclarationRef on every type reference (the resolver-identity item on carrier_alias_refuses_its_own_carriers_constructor) and the predicate becomes a declaration comparison. Every site above is keyed by spelling today and each was closed by joining the algebra row; a sixth consumer that reads the alias roster instead -- any new spelling test, the shape this project has now closed six times over -- re-opens the fork, and the pair #15512 pinned (a canonical-spelled payload against an alias-spelled formal, and the `==` over two records spelled alike but for the carrier) is the canary.",
    "THE SAME ROSTER ANSWERED THE OTHER TWO SET QUESTIONS TOO, AND THEY DISAGREED WITH EACH OTHER. node_is_set_collection asked the alias roster alone, so for a position spelled FinitePowerSet<Int> the three set questions came apart: node_is_collection said element container (after this change), node_is_set_collection said no, and v1.compiler.infer_types type_is_ordered_element_run -- element container and NOT a set collection -- said ordered element run. Two consumers execute that answer: v1.compiler.infer_types ReceiverElementOrSelf, the dual form of the append contract, and v1.compiler.emit_rust rust_append_call_is_concat_form with its is_list_typed_expr test -- so appending one canonical-spelled set onto a list of sets concatenated its MEMBERS into the outer list where the same program spelled `Set` appended ONE element (dag/gunbc/instruments/native_emission_controls.dag control_canonical_set_appended_as_one_element_of_a_list_of_sets beside control_alias_set_appended_as_one_element_of_a_list_of_sets, both built from control_int_set, each holding at 222: one outer element, both members of each inner set surviving). And the set element Ord validation is entered through node_is_set_collection inside render_rust_type_without_applied_binding, so the canonical spelling skipped rust_btree_set_element_ord_eligible outright: a set of a non-Ord declared carrier refused under one spelling and was realized unchecked under the other (dag/test/claim/self_host_emitted_call_target_realization_witness.dag w_alias_set_of_an_ineligible_element_refuses beside w_canonical_set_of_an_ineligible_element_refuses_the_same_way). node_is_set_collection now asks the same algebra row node_is_collection asks, and keeps the element-container shape and arity requirements it already made. THE RESIDUE KEEPS THE RUNG OPEN: the expected-set test in v1.compiler.infer_types expected_type_is_set_collection still carries a `last == \"Set\"` spelling disjunct of its own, and the leaf-name roster still keys the sibling keyed-container read; both fall to the declaration-identity generalization named above.",
    "ONE ROSTER RULE, NOT SEVERAL READS OF IT (gunbc#15539, section 6b root generalization). After the sites above each asked container_template_algebra in their own way, the spelling decision is a single std.types container_spelling_is_algebra(name, algebra); node_is_finite_power_set_algebra_spelling and node_is_keyed_algebra_spelling (04_types), the infer carrier/keyed/set expected-type arms (04_infer spelling_is_collection_carrier, expected_type_is_keyed_collection, expected_type_is_set_collection) and the emitter's FreeMonoid host-container checks (05_emit_rust rust_seed_host_container_base, is_host_text_carrier_type) all call it, so a new spelling is a roster row and not a branch. The literal \"Set\" disjunct in expected_type_is_set_collection is gone. RESIDUE THAT REMAINS: the key is still the authored LEAF SPELLING, here and in keyed_container_has_target_inhabitant (05_emit), which keys on the container name; both retire with the declaration-reference trigger above. The control is native_emission_controls power_set_spelled_seed_*: a FinitePowerSet-spelled empty_set() fold seed crosses the admission (04_infer) and the lowering (#15569), and holds only with both.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_types", decl_name: "node_is_finite_power_set_algebra_spelling"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_types", decl_name: "node_is_collection"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit", decl_name: "v1_type_expr_is_finite_power_set_carrier"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit", decl_name: "v1_field_type_expr_needs_clone_bound_for_param_narrow"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "rust_receiver_has_callable_method_field"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "render_rust_type_without_applied_binding"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "render_rust_fn_sig_type"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_emit_info", decl_name: "field_type_is_realized_container"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_types", decl_name: "node_is_set_collection"),
  ],
}
